670 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 446 0 R
/T (1.1 Prediction Markets: Concept and Function)
/E (1.1 Prediction Markets: Concept and Function)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
671 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 446 0 R
/E (Prediction markets are platforms on which participants buy and sell contracts whose financial value depends on the outcome of a future event. These events can concern a wide range of subjects, including elections, economic indicators, sporting events, public policy decisions, and other developments with uncertain outcomes. Unlike conventional financial markets, where assets generally derive their value from ownership, expected income, or other financial characteristics, prediction markets are organised around the eventual resolution of a specified event. Participants trade according to their expectations about whether a particular outcome will occur. The resulting market prices provide an indication of how participants collectively assess the likelihood of that outcome.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
672 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 446 0 R
/E (The underlying principle of prediction markets is that individuals possess different pieces of information, knowledge, and expectations. One participant may follow political developments closely, another may analyse polling data, while another may respond to campaign events, economic conditions, or information emerging from particular regions. A prediction market provides a mechanism through which these separate assessments can be expressed through trading. Participants who believe that a contract is undervalued may purchase it, while those who consider it overvalued may sell or take an opposing position. As trading takes place, prices adjust according to the supply and demand for the available contracts. The resulting price therefore reflects the market's aggregate assessment, rather than the opinion of any single participant.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
673 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 446 0 R
/E (This process is commonly described as information aggregation. The theoretical appeal of prediction markets lies in their ability to combine dispersed information through a trading mechanism. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
674 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 446 0 R
/E (INTRODUCTION)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
