684 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 447 0 R
/T (Financial incentives may encourage participants to research events, evaluate available information, and act on their expectations. In principle, participants who possess information that is not fully reflected in current prices have an incentive to trade accordingly. Over time, the interaction between buyers and sellers can incorporate information from different sources into a single market price. Wolfers and Zitzewitz \(2006\) describe prediction markets as information markets in which contract prices can be interpreted as market-aggregated forecasts of future events. However, this interpretation depends on the functioning of the market and should not be understood to mean that prices always represent accurate or unbiased probabilities.)
/E (Financial incentives may encourage participants to research events, evaluate available information, and act on their expectations. In principle, participants who possess information that is not fully reflected in current prices have an incentive to trade accordingly. Over time, the interaction between buyers and sellers can incorporate information from different sources into a single market price. Wolfers and Zitzewitz \(2006\) describe prediction markets as information markets in which contract prices can be interpreted as market-aggregated forecasts of future events. However, this interpretation depends on the functioning of the market and should not be understood to mean that prices always represent accurate or unbiased probabilities.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
685 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 447 0 R
/T (1.2 How Prediction-Market Contracts Work)
/E (1.2 How Prediction-Market Contracts Work)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
686 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 447 0 R
/E (A common prediction-market structure is the binary contract, which has two possible outcomes: YES and NO. Each contract is tied to a clearly defined event and its resolution rules. In a conventional $1 binary contract, a YES share pays $1 if the specified event occurs and $0 if it does not. The NO share has the opposite payoff. Participants can purchase either position depending on their expectations about the event.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
687 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 447 0 R
/E (For example, consider a hypothetical contract asking whether a particular political party will win a Senate seat. If the YES share trades at $0.70, its price can be interpreted as an implied market probability of approximately 70%, while the corresponding NO share is associated with approximately 30%. A participant purchasing the YES share at $0.70 would receive $1 if the event resolves in favour of YES, producing a gross gain of $0.30 per share before fees and other costs. If the event resolves as NO, the YES share becomes worthless and the participant loses the amount invested.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
688 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 447 0 R
/E (The relationship between price and probability is one of the defining characteristics of prediction markets. Unlike traditional polls, which generally report percentages of respondents supporting particular candidates or parties, binary prediction-market contracts produce prices that can be interpreted as probabilities of specified outcomes. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
