709 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 449 0 R
/E (Their formation depends on the participants who choose to trade, the information they possess, their willingness to assume financial risk, and the amount of trading activity in a particular market. The number of participants, market liquidity, bid-ask spreads, transaction costs, and the presence of speculative behaviour can all affect the quality and stability of displayed prices. A market price should therefore not be interpreted as a direct reflection of public opinion or as a guarantee of the eventual election outcome.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
710 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 449 0 R
/T (1.4 Prediction Markets and Traditional Opinion Polling)
/E (1.4 Prediction Markets and Traditional Opinion Polling)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
711 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 449 0 R
/E (Although prediction markets and opinion polls can both provide information about an election, they operate through fundamentally different mechanisms. Traditional opinion polls collect responses from a sample of individuals and measure their reported voting intentions or preferences at a particular point in time. Their usefulness depends on factors such as sampling methods, respondent participation, population weighting, question wording, and the accuracy with which the sample represents the electorate.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
712 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 449 0 R
/E (Prediction markets, by contrast, do not attempt to survey the electorate. They aggregate the expectations of market participants through the buying and selling of outcome-based contracts. The participants are not necessarily eligible voters, and their trading decisions are not intended to represent the distribution of political preferences among the population. Instead, market prices reflect the assessments of those willing and able to participate in the market.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
713 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 449 0 R
/E (A further distinction concerns what each source measures. Polls commonly report candidate support as a percentage of respondents, or the difference in support between competing candidates. Prediction markets generally express an assessment of the probability of a specified election outcome. A candidate or party can therefore lead in polling while its associated market contract trades below a high probability of winning. This is not necessarily contradictory: a polling margin measures reported preferences at the time of the survey, whereas a prediction-market contract concerns the eventual outcome of the election. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
