721 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 450 0 R
/E (The market price may incorporate expectations about future developments, uncertainty surrounding the campaign, and information beyond the results of individual polls.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
722 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 450 0 R
/E (Existing academic research has examined the comparative forecasting properties of these two approaches. Wolfers and Zitzewitz \(2006\) discuss the capacity of prediction markets to aggregate dispersed information, while Reade and Vaughan Williams \(2019\) examine how opinion polls can be converted into probabilistic forecasts to allow more meaningful comparisons with prediction markets. Their research highlights that polls and markets do not necessarily measure the same quantity: polls generally provide information about vote shares or preferences, whereas prediction markets provide prices associated with event outcomes. Comparisons between them therefore require attention to the differences in what is being measured.  Earlier work compares polls, markets, experts and models across US elections and finds that the forecast horizon matters \(Vaughan Williams and Reade, 2016\).)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
723 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 450 0 R
/E (This distinction provides the foundation for the present study. Rather than treating prediction-market prices as an alternative form of polling, the study examines them as a separate source of electoral information. By comparing polling margins with Polymarket's Democratic Party YES probabilities across the 2026 United States Senate election cycle, the research investigates how the two sources evolved over time, the extent to which their movements corresponded, and the circumstances in which their assessments differed.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
724 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 450 0 R
/T (1.5 About the Project)
/E (1.5 About the Project)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
725 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 450 0 R
/E (How did traditional polling averages and prediction-market probabilities evolve through the 2026 US Senate cycle, and how did each compare with the eventual results? The study began as a ranking of pollster accuracy, but polling data proved too fragmented for that \(mixed populations and methods, inconsistent quality grades\). It was reframed as a comparison of two public information sources about the same races: published polls and Polymarket prices.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
