828 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 458 0 R
/E (The polling average is the equal-weight mean of survey margins in a trailing window \(21 days in the baseline; 14, 30 and 45 as sensitivity\), dated by fieldwork end date. The end date is used because a midpoint can precede publication and would make the market appear to react to information not yet public.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
829 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 458 0 R
/E (A trailing window often holds a single survey, which is not an average, and many states have long periods with no survey at all \(Section 5.1.1\). Weekly comparisons are therefore also run only for weeks in which the window holds at least k surveys \(k = 1 or 3 for changes; 1, 3 and 5 for levels\). The threshold is a measurement rule set by data density, not by where correlations appear. Days without a poll average are left blank, never interpolated.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
