860 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 460 0 R
/E (The primary sample is the 16 states with at least five usable general-election surveys \(Section 3\). The competitive subgroup is the 12 of those 16 whose market price was between 10% and 90% on at least half of days: Florida, Georgia, Iowa, Kansas, Maine, Michigan, Minnesota, New Hampshire, North Carolina, Ohio, South Carolina and Texas. The other four \(Arkansas, Massachusetts, Montana, Rhode Island\) have prices pinned near 0% or 100%. The 10% to 90% rule was fixed on structural grounds \(can the market move at all\) before any correlation was examined. Because the subgroup is selected on the market's own variability, the 16-state sample is primary.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
861 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 460 0 R
/T (Polling average and market variable)
/E (Polling average and market variable)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
862 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 460 0 R
/E (These quantities are defined in Sections 4.3 and 4.4. In brief: each survey contributes one margin \(Democratic minus Republican, in percentage points\) for the eventual nominee pair; the polling average is the equal-weight mean of survey margins in a trailing window dated by fieldwork end \(21 days in the baseline; 14, 30 and 45 as sensitivity\); and the market variable is the daily mean of the Democratic YES price, analysed as log-odds with prices clipped to 1% to 99% \(0.5% as a sensitivity check\). Days with no survey in the window have no average, and nothing is interpolated. Where a window holds a single survey, the 'average' is that one survey, so several tests require at least k surveys in the window. Analysis windows end on Nov 2, 2026 \(UTC\).)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
