/E (We do not treat this as evidence that markets react with a delay. Adjacent weekly changes in a 21-day average share two-thirds of their data, so the poll-change series is strongly autocorrelated, the effective sample is smaller than n, and a naive test \(r = 0.156 on 209 observations\) overstates the evidence. Polls are dated by fieldwork end, but the public sees them days later \(a median of 3 days in Analysis 3\), so a market that reacts instantly at publication would still appear to follow the poll change by about a week. The four-week test gives no support for polls leading markets. The lead-lag pattern remains exploratory, and Analysis 3 uses publication times to examine it directly.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [2]
>>
