/E (The slope is positive in every specification, but the result is not robust. Table 3 shows the strong pattern that appears when polls are dense \(p of 0.001 to 0.005 at three and five surveys\). Table 4 shows that it weakens once the standard errors are t-based and the sample is put on a common calendar \(k = 5, state effects only: p = 0.35, with the standard error doubling\). Calendar-week effects, which remove shared trends, leave slopes between +0.006 and +0.070 with p-values from 0.077 to 0.79.)
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1009 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 474 0 R
/E (The all-16 result at three surveys also depends on how the saturated states are clipped. With a 0.5% and 99.5% clip it rises from +0.029 to +0.055 \(p = 0.006\) with state effects, and to +0.059 \(p = 0.077\) with week effects. The competitive sample is unaffected. The dense-poll weeks are concentrated in September and October, when both series trend, and there are only 12 to 16 clusters. We therefore read the levels result as suggestive, not as an established within-state relationship.)
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
