/P 476 0 R
/E (Levels and changes measure different things. Levels across states capture large, slow differences \(partisanship, incumbency, the general environment\) that polls and markets both see. Changes require measuring small movements. Week-to-week changes in a poll average are dominated by which surveys enter or leave the window: a single survey margin in this sample has a median standard error of 3.3 points \(computed from each survey's own shares and sample size, assuming simple random sampling\), so the difference between two thin averages is mostly noise.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1032 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 476 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1033 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 476 0 R
/E (Two explanations fit. Markets may respond only weakly to short-run changes in poll averages. Or those changes may be mostly noise that biases the slope toward zero, an effect that shrinks as the horizon lengthens. This design cannot separate the two, so the finding is an absence of detectable short-run co-movement, not evidence that markets ignore polls.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1034 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 476 0 R
/E (Link to Analysis 3. Analysis 3 \(Section 5.3\) finds a day-level response of about +0.016 log-odds per point of surprise around poll releases, similar in size to the weekly slopes here. Both use the same prices and polls, so this is consistency, not independent confirmation.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1035 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 476 0 R
/E (What Analysis 1 does not show. It does not show causation, does not say which source is more accurate \(Analysis 4\), and does not identify when and where the two disagree \(Analysis 2\).)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [6]
>>
