1124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 483 0 R
/T (The tie probability measures any bias common to all states. From Sep 19 it is 41.0% to 44.5% \(39.9% on Nov 1\), not 50%. At slopes of 0.35 to 0.39 this is equivalent to shifting the poll margin by 0.6 to 1.1 points toward the Republican. Because the benchmark is fitted across the same states, this offset is the only place a bias common to all states can show up. The state-by-state gaps below are measured around the fitted line, so they do not contain it. The offset is consistent with the market pricing in state partisanship, or with a house effect in poll averages, and this analysis cannot say which. Analysis 4 compares it with the results.)
