1166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 487 0 R
/E (The main finding is agreement: once the benchmark is not distorted by saturated states, the market price is explained closely by the poll margin, and most gaps are small: 85% of state-weeks are within 3 margin points, about the sampling error of a single survey \(Section 5.1.9\).)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 487 0 R
/E (Where gaps remain \(Minnesota and Kansas\), two explanations fit. The market may add information the poll average lacks, such as a prior about state partisanship, candidate quality, turnout or the national environment. Or the poll averages are noisy and carry house effects, so a common slope fits imperfectly. The common offset has the same two readings: the market may have expected polls to overstate Democrats, or it may carry a general Republican prior. This analysis cannot tell these apart. A second regressor for state partisanship, such as the 2024 presidential margin, would partly separate them and is a candidate extension.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
