1202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 490 0 R
/E (Question. When an individual poll is released, do market prices move in the direction of what it says?)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 490 0 R
/E (Approach. Analysis 1 related changes in 21-day poll averages to changes in prices. A single release can carry more news than the average it joins, so Analysis 3 looks at individual surveys. Each release is an event. Its surprise is how far the survey's margin sits from what earlier surveys in the same state had already shown. If prices react, a poll above the prior average should be followed by a rise in the market's log-odds of a Democratic win, and a poll below it by a fall. Publication times come from source URLs, which limits the test to a day-level window on 69 events.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 490 0 R
/E (Key findings:)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 490 0 R
/T (5.3 ANALYSIS 3: DO PRICES MOVE WHEN A POLL IS RELEASED?)
/E (5.3 ANALYSIS 3: DO PRICES MOVE WHEN A POLL IS RELEASED?)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 490 0 R
/E (Reading the results. Prices moved with the surprise of a poll in the days around its release, and the pattern does not appear in windows before the release, so it is not a trend that was already under way. )
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
