/T (Poll-implied probabilities scored better than market prices at every horizon, at every assumed SD and in both samples: 0.051 to 0.066 against 0.080 on the final day, and 0.077 to 0.093 against 0.139 thirty days out. The gap comes from the close races the Democrats won: at 30 days the market priced a Democratic win below the poll-implied probability in Kansas \(33.7% against 68.7%\), South Carolina \(13.8% against 41.7%\), Iowa \(42.2% against 49.4%\) and Ohio \(61.1% against 69.1%\), while in Texas \(64.2% against 61.6%\) and Maine \(59.4% against 60.2%\) the two were close. )
/E (Poll-implied probabilities scored better than market prices at every horizon, at every assumed SD and in both samples: 0.051 to 0.066 against 0.080 on the final day, and 0.077 to 0.093 against 0.139 thirty days out. The gap comes from the close races the Democrats won: at 30 days the market priced a Democratic win below the poll-implied probability in Kansas \(33.7% against 68.7%\), South Carolina \(13.8% against 41.7%\), Iowa \(42.2% against 49.4%\) and Ohio \(61.1% against 69.1%\), while in Texas \(64.2% against 61.6%\) and Maine \(59.4% against 60.2%\) the two were close. )
