1449 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 510 0 R
/T (In the 15 races that were head-to-head contests between a Democrat and a Republican, the Democrats outperformed the final poll average in every state: the mean signed error is +4.52 points, the mean absolute error 4.52 and the root-mean-square error 5.26. Arkansas \(+0.6\) and Kansas \(+0.8\) are within one point of zero, so the count of 15 of 15 is sensitive to late-count shifts while the average is not. The errors run from +0.6 \(Arkansas\) to +9.1 \(Texas\). Density did not protect: Texas missed by 9.1 with 25 polls in the window and Georgia by 8.9 with 6, against Michigan by 2.0 with 18 and Kansas by 0.8 with 8. Rhode Island \(1 poll\), Montana \(2\), Arkansas \(2\) and Massachusetts \(3\) rest on very few polls.)
/E (In the 15 races that were head-to-head contests between a Democrat and a Republican, the Democrats outperformed the final poll average in every state: the mean signed error is +4.52 points, the mean absolute error 4.52 and the root-mean-square error 5.26. Arkansas \(+0.6\) and Kansas \(+0.8\) are within one point of zero, so the count of 15 of 15 is sensitive to late-count shifts while the average is not. The errors run from +0.6 \(Arkansas\) to +9.1 \(Texas\). Density did not protect: Texas missed by 9.1 with 25 polls in the window and Georgia by 8.9 with 6, against Michigan by 2.0 with 18 and Kansas by 0.8 with 8. Rhode Island \(1 poll\), Montana \(2\), Arkansas \(2\) and Massachusetts \(3\) rest on very few polls.)
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1450 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 510 0 R
/T (Because one national shift moves many states together, these 15 errors are closer to one error than to 15 independent ones, so we report the average bias and not an interval. The common shift \(the mean error\) accounts for 74% of the mean squared error, leaving a standard deviation of 2.7 points around it.)
/E (Because one national shift moves many states together, these 15 errors are closer to one error than to 15 independent ones, so we report the average bias and not an interval. The common shift \(the mean error\) accounts for 74% of the mean squared error, leaving a standard deviation of 2.7 points around it.)
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
