/Title (What the results do and do not show. Taken together, polls and prediction markets look complementary. They carry much the same information about which races favour which party, and they differ at the margin, in close races and in how they price uncertainty. Identifying a likely winner is not the same as assigning a well-calibrated probability, and a source that scores better in one election cannot be called the better forecaster in general. The study covers one cycle and 12 to 16 states whose errors move together, the polls were equal-weighted with no house-effect adjustment, the results are provisional until certified, and several tests were added after earlier results were seen. The 2026 Senate races are a useful case study, not a ranking of polls against markets.)
/Dest [99 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 7916 0 R
/Next 7474 0 R
>>
endobj
