/Title (Financial incentives may encourage participants to research events, evaluate available information, and act on their expectations. In principle, participants who possess information that is not fully reflected in current prices have an incentive to trade accordingly. Over time, the interaction between buyers and sellers can incorporate information from different sources into a single market price. Wolfers and Zitzewitz \(2006\) describe prediction markets as information markets in which contract prices can be interpreted as market-aggregated forecasts of future events. However, this interpretation depends on the functioning of the market and should not be understood to mean that prices always represent accurate or unbiased probabilities.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7903 0 R
/Prev 7941 0 R
/First 7942 0 R
/Last 7943 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 7914 0 R
>>
