/Title (Forecast evaluation. On the final day, polls and markets each named 13 of the 15 head-to-head winners, both missing Iowa and South Carolina, which the Democrats won. The market named 31 of all 34 scored winners. When poll margins were converted to probabilities with an assumed normal forecast error \(SD of 4, 5 or 6 points\), polls scored lower Brier scores than market prices at every horizon. That advantage depends on the assumed conversion and on an election in which the Democrats outperformed the polls. Reading the poll average 3 to 5 days earlier, to allow for publication delay, narrowed the final-day advantage to near parity \(0.072 against 0.080 at a 5-day lag\) but did not reverse it.)
/Dest [98 0 R /Fit]
