8017 0 obj
<<
/Title (The tie probability measures any bias common to all states. From Sep 19 it is 41.0% to 44.5% \(39.9% on Nov 1\), not 50%. At slopes of 0.35 to 0.39 this is equivalent to shifting the poll margin by 0.6 to 1.1 points toward the Republican. Because the benchmark is fitted across the same states, this offset is the only place a bias common to all states can show up. The state-by-state gaps below are measured around the fitted line, so they do not contain it. The offset is consistent with the market pricing in state partisanship, or with a house effect in poll averages, and this analysis cannot say which. Analysis 4 compares it with the results.)
/Dest [53 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7998 0 R
/Prev 8016 0 R
/Next 8007 0 R
>>
endobj
8018 0 obj
<<
/Title (No state meets the persistent-disagreement rule as set \(a gap of 3 or more margin points in at least 60% of weeks\). The result depends on the two cutoffs \(Table 8\). Minnesota qualifies if the required share is relaxed to 50%, at either a 3-point or a 4-point threshold. At a 2-point threshold Kansas and Minnesota qualify at shares of 60% and 70%, and South Carolina joins them at 50%. Under the rule as set, Minnesota comes closest, with a gap of 3 or more margin points in 5 of 9 weeks \(56%\). Across all 107 state-weeks, 85% have a gap smaller than 3 margin points, and in 11 of the 12 states the mean gap is within 2.3 margin points.)
/Dest [55 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 8008 0 R
/Next 8026 0 R
>>
endobj
8019 0 obj
<<
/Title (Poll-implied probabilities scored better than market prices at every horizon, at every assumed SD and in both samples: 0.051 to 0.066 against 0.080 on the final day, and 0.077 to 0.093 against 0.139 thirty days out. The gap comes from the close races the Democrats won: at 30 days the market priced a Democratic win below the poll-implied probability in Kansas \(33.7% against 68.7%\), South Carolina \(13.8% against 41.7%\), Iowa \(42.2% against 49.4%\) and Ohio \(61.1% against 69.1%\), while in Texas \(64.2% against 61.6%\) and Maine \(59.4% against 60.2%\) the two were close.)
